>> title: Hong Kong Milk Tea
>> category: Drinks
>> tags: tea, hong kong, evaporated milk, brewed, drink
>> counters: Cha Chaan Teng
>> aka: hong kong milk tea, 港式奶茶, 奶茶, gong sik naai cha, naai cha, lai cha, nai cha, silk stocking milk tea, 絲襪奶茶, si mat naai cha, HK milk tea, milk tea, stocking tea, hk style milk tea
>> servings: 4
>> time: 25 min
>> slack: narrow — the leaf keeps working while it sits, and tea left past the steep comes out bitter under the milk rather than strong
>> step.2: blend the two cuts, into the bag
>> step.3: simmer 2 to 3 min at 90-96°C
>> step.4: pour it back through the bag, 3 to 6 pulls, steep 6 min
>> step.5: pour the milk in first, tea on top, 7 to 3

No two shops blend it the same — Hong Kong's heritage listing for it says outright that there is no standard.

Tip @Ceylon tea bags{6}(cut open; the fine cut, 65% of the blend) and @loose-leaf Ceylon black tea{7%g}(the coarse 35%, and the aroma) into a #cloth tea bag{}, or a fine strainer you can pour through.

Bring @water{2 1/2%cup}(600 mL) to 90-96°C in a #saucepan{}, drop @&(~1)blended leaf{} in, and ~simmer{2-3%min} until it is dark and just moving.

Lift @&(~1)bag{} clear and pour the tea back through it onto the leaves, three to six times, then cover and ~steep{6%min}.

Pour @evaporated milk{3/4%cup}(180 mL) and @granulated sugar{4%tsp}(to taste) into warm cups and send @&(~1)pulled tea{} in on top.
